\@writefile{tdo}{\contentsline {todo}{{\color@box {}{red!40}{\leavevmode {\color  {red!40}o}}\ mentioning GE's `substantive propositions' here - worth cross-reffing with wherever I end up writing them up in detail.}}{33}}
\@writefile{tdo}{\contentsline {todo}{{\color@box {}{red!40}{\leavevmode {\color  {red!40}o}}\ What I want to argue somewhere: current thought on transition and energy is offering a whole bag of propositions. What I want theory to do is to give us substantive propositions that confirm or deny the statements made, or offer interesting new ones that nevertheless might shed light on those issues. GE deals with a very different proposition 'base'. It's all about trying to explain the core-periphery pattern as 'an endogenous process...' Is it really any good at answering the kind of questions I'm interested in?}}{33}}
